{"The limit can be evaluated using the Taylor series expansions for $\tan(x)$ and $\arctan(x)$.

$$
\lim_{x \to 0}x \tan (xa+ \arctan \frac{b}{x})": 0, "= \lim_{x \to 0}x \frac{b \cos (a x)+x \sin (a x)}{x \cos (a x)-b \sin (a x)} $$ Using Taylor series expansions, we have $$ \begin{aligned} \cos(a x) &= 1 - \frac{a^2 x^2}{2} + O(x^4) \\ \sin(a x) &= a x - \frac{a^3 x^3}{6} + O(x^5) \end{aligned} $$ Substituting these into the expression for the limit, we get $$ \begin{aligned} \lim_{x \to 0}x \frac{b": 0, "\left(1 - \frac{a^2 x^2}{2}\right)+x^2 \left(a - \frac{a^3 x}{6}\right)}{x \left(1 - \frac{a^2 x^2}{2}\right)-b \left(a x - \frac{a^3 x^3}{6}\right)} \end{aligned} $$ Long division yields $$ \begin{aligned} \frac{b}{1-ab} + \frac{a (a b (a b-3)+3)}{3 (1-ab)^2}x^2 + O(x^4) \end{aligned} $$ If $ab=1$, the limit is undefined because of the term $x^2$. If $ab \neq 1$, the limit simplifies to $$ \frac{b}{1-ab} $$ This is the final result.": 0}

