{"To prove that $\binom{k}{p} \equiv \left\lfloor \frac{k}{p} \right\rfloor \pmod p$ for all odd prime number $p$ and $k\ge 3$, we can use Lucas' theorem (Document [1]).": 0, "We express $k$ and $p$ in base $p$ and compute $\binom{k}{p}$ as the product of binomial coefficients of their base-$p$ digits.": 0, "Since $k\ge 3$, the base-$p$ representation of $k$ has at least one digit other than $0$. Let $k_i$ denote the $i$-th digit of the base-$p$ representation of $k$, where $i\ge 0$.": 0, "Then, we have: \[ \binom{k}{p} = \prod_{i \ge 0} \binom{k_i}{p} \pmod{p} \] Now, we consider the base-$p$ representation of $p$. Since $p$ is an odd prime, its base-$p$ representation consists of only $0$s and $1$s.": 0, "Let $p_i$ denote the $i$-th digit of the base-$p$ representation of $p$, where $i\ge 0$.": 0, "Then, we have: \[ \binom{p_i}{p} = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } p_i = 0 \\ 0 & \text{if } p_i = 1 \end{cases} \pmod{p} \] Since $k\ge 3$, there must exist an index $j$ such that $k_j \neq p_j$.": 0, "Without loss of generality, let us assume that $k_j = 1$ and $p_j = 0$.": 0, "Then, we have: \[ \binom{k}{p} = \prod_{i \ge 0} \binom{k_i}{p} \equiv \binom{1}{p} \prod_{i \neq j} \binom{k_i}{p} \pmod{p} \] Since $\binom{1}{p} = 1$, we have: \[ \binom{k}{p} \equiv \prod_{i \neq j} \binom{k_i}{p} \pmod{p} \] Now, we note that $\prod_{i \neq j} \binom{k_i}{p} \equiv \left\lfloor \frac{k}{p} \right\rfloor \pmod{p}$, as the product is the number of ways to choose $p$ items from the set of base-$p$ digits": 0, "of $k$, excluding the digit $p_j = 0$.": 0, "Since $\binom{k}{p}$ is congruent to $\left\lfloor \frac{k}{p} \right\rfloor$ modulo $p$, we have:

\[
\binom{k}{p} \equiv \left\lfloor \frac{k}{p} \right\rfloor \pmod{p}
\]

This completes the proof.": 0}

