where $f(m)=\left(\dfrac12\right)^m\sum_{r=0}^na_rm^r$ and $a_r$ are arbitrary constants.": 0, "Using this identity, we can rewrite the given sequence as: $$\sum_{r=1}^\infty\binom{r+3}3\left(\dfrac12\right)^{r-1}=f(4)-f(1)$ Now, we can use the given identity to find $f(m)$: $$f(m)=\left(\dfrac12\right)^m\sum_{r=0}^na_rm^r$$ Comparing the coefficients of $m^3,$ we have: $$\dfrac16=\dfrac{a_3}4-\dfrac{a_3}2\iff a_3=?$$ Comparing the coefficients of $m^2,$ we have: $$1=\dfrac{a_2+3a_3}4-\dfrac{a_2}2\iff a_2=?$$ Similarly, comparing the coefficients of $m$ and the constants, we can find $a_1,a_0$. Using Telescoping Series, we have: $$\sum_{r=1}^\infty\binom{r+3}3\left(\dfrac12\right)^{r-1}=-f(1)$$ Now, we can find $f(1)$": 0, "using the known values of $a_r$: $$f(1)=\left(\dfrac12\right)^1\sum_{r=0}^na_1(1)^r=a_1$$ Thus, $$\sum_{r=1}^\infty\binom{r+3}3\left(\dfrac12\right)^{r-1}=-a_1$$ Using the given identity, we can find the sum of the given sequence: $$\sum_{r=1}^\infty\binom{r+3}3\left(\dfrac12\right)^{r-1}=f(4)-f(1)=(1/2)^4\sum_{r=0}^na_4(4)^r-(1/2)\sum_{r=0}^na_1(1)^r$$ Now, we can find the sum of the given sequence by finding the values of $a_1,a_4$ and substituting them into the above equation.": 0}

