2)": 0, "The sequence $a_n = \frac{1\cdot 3\cdot…\cdot(2n-1)}{2\cdot4\cdot…\cdot2n}$ is decreasing because it is the product of $(1-1/2), (1-1/4),…,(1-1/2n)$, which is always less than $1$. To show that it is bounded from below, note that $a_n > \frac{1}{2n+1}$ for all $n \ge 1$.": 0, "This can be shown by cross-multiplying and using the inequality $\left(\frac{2k-1}{2k}\right)^2 \le \frac{2k-1}{2k+1}$ for $k \ge 1$.": 0, "The lower bound of the sequence is $0$.

